    "value": "4 AGVs",
    "text": "Four AGVs is the smallest fleet that keeps both cranes nearly always busy. The uncertainty experiments use this fleet, because with more AGVs the spare vehicles hide most dispatching mistakes.",
    "conditions": "No randomness · 2 to 12 AGVs · 30 instances",
    "ref": "E1"
   },
   {
    "value": "3.6 → 7.1 s",
    "text": "Random crane times roughly double the crane idle time per container, for every rule.",
    "conditions": "4 AGVs · crane cycle 75 s ± 40% · 30 seeds",
    "ref": "E3"
   },
   {
    "value": "−21% → +18%",
    "text": "A schedule optimised in advance beats the best rule when nothing is random, but loses to it once crane times vary.",
    "conditions": "Strict plan vs Briskorn rule · 4 AGVs · ±0% and ±40% · 30 seeds",
    "ref": "E3b"
   },
   {
    "value": "≥ 26%",
    "text": "A dispatcher that knew all future crane times would cut the idle time of every rule by at least a quarter, so a better real-time dispatcher is possible.",
